WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.920
Assume there is a circulant Hadamard
matrix of order four u squared,

00:00:03.920 --> 00:00:09.400
with u odd and greater than one. Let I be
the set of distinct prime divisors of u.

00:00:09.880 --> 00:00:14.720
For a subset S, let zeta sub S be the
product of its chosen prime roots,

00:00:14.720 --> 00:00:17.550
with the empty product equal to one.

00:00:18.140 --> 00:00:22.000
The preceding polynomials
define one element, script S:

00:00:22.400 --> 00:00:27.580
the alternating sum of E at zeta
sub S divided by c at zeta sub S.

00:00:28.390 --> 00:00:31.180
Two proof branches now
meet at this same element.

00:00:31.760 --> 00:00:37.820
Node seven's product-to-sum argument gives
script S residue zero modulo one plus i.

00:00:38.200 --> 00:00:41.540
Node eight point three's
all-ones calculation gives

00:00:41.540 --> 00:00:45.540
script S residue one modulo one plus i.

00:00:46.100 --> 00:00:51.400
These conclusions come from the same
assumed matrix and the same subset inputs.

00:00:52.200 --> 00:00:54.980
Both statements live in
the same quotient ring,

00:00:55.480 --> 00:01:01.020
A modulo the ideal generated by one
plus i. Here A is the integers with i,

00:01:01.500 --> 00:01:05.220
the selected prime roots,
and one over u adjoined.

00:01:06.080 --> 00:01:08.940
Node six proved that
this quotient is nonzero.

00:01:09.460 --> 00:01:11.480
Its zero and its identity are distinct.

00:01:12.280 --> 00:01:15.900
Every evaluated denominator c
sub S is a unit before reduction,

00:01:15.900 --> 00:01:18.220
and remains a unit afterward.

00:01:18.220 --> 00:01:22.780
The normalized expressions are
therefore defined in this one ring.

00:01:23.680 --> 00:01:25.920
Bring the two values of script S together.

00:01:26.600 --> 00:01:31.140
They would force zero to equal one. That
is impossible in the nonzero quotient.

00:01:31.540 --> 00:01:34.860
Characteristic two makes
one plus one equal zero;

00:01:35.340 --> 00:01:37.180
it does not make one equal zero.

00:01:37.600 --> 00:01:41.220
We have reached a contradiction to
the assumed circulant Hadamard matrix.

00:01:41.980 --> 00:01:45.030
This excludes every
odd u greater than one,

00:01:45.040 --> 00:01:48.760
rather than only the
guiding case u equals three.

00:01:49.140 --> 00:01:52.220
The full subset calculation
supplies the general conclusion.

00:01:53.120 --> 00:01:55.640
The argument does not
exclude u equals one.

00:01:56.160 --> 00:01:59.060
The delta theorem used earlier
requires u greater than one,

00:01:59.060 --> 00:02:02.560
so its odd-order conclusion
cannot be applied at u equals one.

00:02:02.960 --> 00:02:05.100
The next step combines this exclusion with

00:02:05.100 --> 00:02:08.620
the order restriction and checks
the two remaining matrix orders.
